 For a GGUF without a `nextn` block, omit `--speculative-config`; the backbone
 loads normally without MTP.
 
+## Performance tuning
+
+Quantized matmuls go through fused kernels that read the packed weight
+directly. That is the right choice for decode, where each step has few rows.
+A prefill chunk has thousands of rows and is compute bound, and there
+dequantizing the weight and running a dense GEMM can be several times faster.
+
+Set `VLLM_GGUF_DENSE_GEMM_MIN_ROWS` to route matmuls with at least that many
+input rows through the dense path and keep the fused kernels below it:
+
+```bash
+VLLM_GGUF_DENSE_GEMM_MIN_ROWS=1024 vllm serve Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B-GGUF:Q8_0   --tokenizer Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B
+```
+
+The row count of a matmul is the number of tokens in the engine step, so pick
+a value above `--max-num-seqs` and at or below `--max-num-batched-tokens`.
+The dense path materializes one dequantized weight at a time, so it needs
+transient GPU memory for the largest quantized layer. The default `0` keeps
+the fused kernels for every batch size.
+
 ## Tested model coverage
 
 The plugin uses vLLM's model implementations and a generic GGUF weight
